{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdyCe8rUuEGVhGypLpTJ4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Fix Plan\n\n## 1. **Declare the numeric contract in `golden/mapper.ts`**\n   Modify the `irr` field serializer to round to **1e-10 dollars** (one order of magnitude tighter than the stated 1e-9 tolerance, per the doctrine: \"coarse enough to absorb environment noise, fine enough that genuine behavioral change still fails\").\n   \n   ```typescript\n   // In golden/mapper.ts, irr serialization:\n   irr: Math.round(record.irr * 1e10) / 1e10\n   ```\n\n## 2. **Regenerate the baseline on Node 20**\n   ```bash\n   nvm use 20\n   npm test -- --update-golden\n   ```\n   Commit the updated `golden/projections.json` with a message documenting the decision: \"Round IRR to 1e-10 in golden mapper to absorb cross-runtime FP noise while preserving genuine behavioral divergence (tolerance: 1e-9).\"\n\n## 3. **Verify the fix on both runtimes**\n   ```bash\n   nvm use 20 && npm test -- --golden    # must pass (baseline matches itself)\n   nvm use 22 && npm test -- --golden    # must pass (deltas < 1e-10 absorbed)\n   ```\n\n## Why this works\n- The doctrine prohibits pinning the runtime; instead, the **comparator** (mapper) expresses the actual promised precision.\n- The 1e-14–2e-13 deltas are FP noise from Newton iteration across Node versions, not genuine divergence in the projection algorithm.\n- Rounding at 1e-10 absorbs that noise while keeping 1e-9 tolerance meaningful for real bugs.\n- Regenerating on Node 20 proves the baseline is self-consistent; passing on Node 22 proves the contract is portable as claimed."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":689,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":446,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}}
